{
  "requirement": "Each documented profile defines which grammar nodes are comments; the Markdown\nprofile's comments are HTML comment blocks, so an inline HTML comment within a\nparagraph remains ordinary paragraph text. In every supported language, comments on consecutive lines with no other source\nbetween them MUST form one comment run. A comment that follows other source on\nthe same line, including another comment, MUST form a run by itself and MUST belong to the innermost outline node\ncontaining that line. Any other run MUST belong to the next outline node that\nstarts after it within the smallest node containing it; when that container has\nno such node, the run MUST belong to the container, which is the root node at top\nlevel. A profile MAY bind a comment kind to its container\ninstead: such a run MUST belong to the smallest node containing it. The Rust\nprofile MUST bind inner doc comments (`//!`, `/*!`) to their container, so a\nfile-level inner doc comment belongs to the root node. A reference in a comment MUST count for the node its run belongs to under\n{cite}`requirements.references.coverage.tests` and\n{cite}`requirements.references.authoring_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests cover each obligation in `requirements.references.outline_comments`. They assert outcomes, not just execution.\n\n**Profile-defined comments and Markdown**\n- `markdown_comments_are_html_comment_blocks_not_inline_comments` asserts three things: only the block counts as a comment, an inline `<!-- -->` is not a comment, and the line is owned by a `paragraph` node.\n- `comment_runs_follow_the_same_rules_in_every_language` also covers Markdown block comments, both before and after a section.\n\n**Consecutive-line comments form a run**\n- `comment_between_two_fns_belongs_to_the_next` and `comment_runs_belong_to_the_next_node_or_their_container` cover multi-line runs.\n- `comment_fences_close_within_one_comment_run` in `src/fenced.rs` shows run boundaries have an observable effect. A fence closes within one run, a blank-line-separated later run does not close it, and a multi-line block comment forms one run.\n- `distinct_runs_with_the_same_owner_do_not_close_each_others_fences` covers the break between an inner doc comment and an ordinary comment, and the break after a trailing opener.\n\n**Comment after other source on its line**\n- `comment_after_code_on_a_line_belongs_to_that_lines_node` covers trailing comments after `fn a() {}`, inside a body, after a closing brace, and after `mod` closes.\n- `a_comment_after_another_comment_on_its_line_forms_its_own_run` covers the comment-after-comment case. The first comment goes to `fn[b]` and the second to the root, because it is the innermost node containing that line.\n- `comment_runs_belong_to_the_next_node_or_their_container` includes the trailing case.\n\n**Next node, container, and root fallback**\n- The main Rust test covers each owner case:\n  - a run before a node goes to that node;\n  - a floating run goes to the container;\n  - a run after the last node in `mod[m]` goes to `mod[m]`;\n  - a top-level tail goes to the root.\n- `trailing_comment_after_last_fn_in_mod_belongs_to_the_mod` and `top_level_comment_with_nothing_after_belongs_to_the_root` repeat the container and root cases.\n- `comment_runs_follow_the_same_rules_in_every_language` repeats these rules for Python, TS, TSX, CSS, TOML, YAML and Markdown.\n- `yaml_comments_before_a_key_belong_to_that_key_not_the_previous_one` and `workflow_job_marks_belong_to_the_job_that_follows` cover YAML ownership, including the real workflow files.\n\n**Rust inner doc comments bind to their container**\n- `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` checks `//!` and `/*!` at file level, and both go to the root.\n- The main Rust test checks `//! Module docs.` inside `mod m` and expects `mod[m]`, even though a later `fn` follows.\n\n**A reference in a comment counts for its run's node**\n- Under `coverage.tests`, `test_functions_need_a_test_reference` covers:\n  - an attached comment before `#[test]`;\n  - a reference in the body;\n  - a closed fence;\n  - an unclosed fence;\n  - a cite-only or impl-only comment that does not satisfy the requirement.\n- Under `authoring_inputs`, `references_contribute_their_owning_outline_nodes` checks that a `{fixme}` fence comment contributes to `fn[gap]`.\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors`, which is not marked for this requirement, asserts that a `{test}` comment before `fn test_ready` contributes `fn[test_ready]`.\n- The consumer-side tests are `search_returns_innermost_nodes_and_comment_owners`, `matches_belong_to_the_innermost_node_or_comment_owner`, and `search_ranges_resolve_to_comment_owners_or_the_common_enclosing_item`.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. The `bundle_combines_...` test lacks a `{test}` marking for this requirement, but the marked tests already cover the obligation adequately, so I did not count it as a gap.",
  "references": "138753463260eee8add23dd1af24e539e64711ac7c8179561e26d632d8e603a6",
